#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b435961a-37d4-5224-ba98-809edcd5fcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349958} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d04f0e-ce33-560f-8d25-2455198c422e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349959} {:answer/id #uuid "b6629edf-3d57-548c-9714-d3922342714c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349960} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6b3535-dc3e-5474-8344-c9298e897403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349961} {:answer/id #uuid "24ddd589-010f-5889-9e7d-826aef99668e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e6b3535-dc3e-5474-8344-c9298e897403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349961} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef2c23a-5001-5982-b0f4-845efea30bc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2ae4a2f-fd5f-5872-89b2-269b37fd8b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-11981" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=4 \\\\ 2x-y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a180dc90-c6b9-577e-9eef-ed60ecafe4e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4d3a07-a231-521c-a426-1cd6cd9993d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349965} {:answer/id #uuid "4b008d64-98cd-5cf1-970e-5ef9d2d8acba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349966} {:answer/id #uuid "739b2b03-cec2-56b5-aa38-4ace4bc119c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349967} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd67f70-d6c2-5071-b7e4-83c5f5974917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349968} {:answer/id #uuid "84139496-dd2c-5b40-a558-029ab7fa76e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b008d64-98cd-5cf1-970e-5ef9d2d8acba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349966} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe4347c-0b67-51e7-a55f-d6c7fdd8f093" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48581854-707a-55c3-820d-b972a1199e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-121028" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi ,\\,x=a\\pi +n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{7}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fde7ef-7f23-585a-b164-e9d1afdcb361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349972} {:answer/id #uuid "53025eaa-e03c-5a79-9f02-e480ce97b82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349973} {:answer/id #uuid "c9864dd8-11c3-550d-9dac-a1977ebd9c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349974} {:answer/id #uuid "15c9a850-725f-517a-bc3d-861d44c558ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349975} {:answer/id #uuid "27415501-96b9-5193-84c6-f9cb710b1f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53025eaa-e03c-5a79-9f02-e480ce97b82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349973} :answer-field/id #uuid "1afb4ca5-ca86-55ee-a9f9-e8dc94611ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e4bfc1-b5ef-51c2-a12a-529f7a844a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-129223" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$   \n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The intervals $[-1,1]$ and $[0,1]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[-1,1]$  and $[0,1].$\n\n- The interval $[-2,2]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00271db1-ce5d-5870-8ecf-b3d687ff0c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4g}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349979} {:answer/id #uuid "4b40eec7-3fcc-5c08-b260-edea6a6b44ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349980} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3cdc3-89a3-56cd-8306-b29ea372c374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349981} {:answer/id #uuid "88dde628-02bf-5a93-b478-e0e0085e04a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349982} {:answer/id #uuid "d12cb98d-88b6-589d-8446-4a8b8393d644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8g}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d12cb98d-88b6-589d-8446-4a8b8393d644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8g}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349983} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed517a1f-0582-5bc2-a1f9-34e84174f8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53d8f5ac-4e03-5656-9b30-96c599c40d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14432" :question/problem "$\\frac{4}{5g}÷\\frac{3}{10{g}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5g}÷\\frac{3}{10{g}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5g}÷\\frac{3}{10{g}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5g}\\cdot \\frac{10{g}^{2}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot \\cancel{g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2\\cdot {g}^{\\cancel{2}}}{3} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{2g}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8g}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f9bd8-9edf-5643-8cdb-a821b6dce09c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15bc5f6-b521-570b-977c-4ec6cb64f4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349986} {:answer/id #uuid "4d474441-f83d-5751-960e-48d3a449323e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8ec8fd-ce2d-5eb3-8db9-45c6f17be030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349988} {:answer/id #uuid "a3271490-7360-5c33-8f48-b80e2a4eb53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349989} {:answer/id #uuid "be1edce8-9456-501a-beba-6ccc30f01c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a8ec8fd-ce2d-5eb3-8db9-45c6f17be030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349988} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb7b41d-1ff5-5dcf-b3d4-1f415821afa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c7ac090-4865-5789-a9b9-f3f3d9345ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-15459" :question/problem "A circle in the Cartesian plane is given by the equation ${y}^{2}+2x+{x}^{2}=24y-120.$ If the circle is written in the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ then $a+b+r=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x+{x}^{2} & =24y-120 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}-24y & =-120 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2x)+({y}^{2}-24y) & =-120 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}) & =-120+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}) & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-12)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-12)^{2} & ={5}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1,b=12,r=5,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+r=-1+12+5=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7065de-d470-5535-99c9-4f47f942c692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2h^{-1}(x) + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117259} {:answer/id #uuid "ec24ec2e-c323-595a-ab8c-e681b59b545a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{h^{-1}(x)} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117260} {:answer/id #uuid "fb195b1f-60ab-54bc-86dc-42681ea6ec21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(\\sqrt{2h^{-1}(x) + 3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117261} {:answer/id #uuid "4b958543-2028-5872-b13e-1dcc29cd0382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2h^{-1}(x) + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117262} {:answer/id #uuid "6cde825d-f19c-5419-b744-d99fde5e49fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2\\sqrt{2h^{-1}(x) + 3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b958543-2028-5872-b13e-1dcc29cd0382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2h^{-1}(x) + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117262} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8d28e3-abc5-5b17-98de-8f55ce76bef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "897cf11f-84a6-5f25-bcaf-38671de37328" :question/math-academy-id "q-32586" :question/problem "If $h(x)=\\sqrt{2x+3},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\sqrt{2x+3},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2y+3}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt{2y+3}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(2y+3)^{1/2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =(2y+3)^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2y+3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{2y+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+3} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{2{h}^{-1}(x)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca74564e-978c-5d2a-9612-2fc106534d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349993} {:answer/id #uuid "5df22c97-e35e-5d21-a311-faa243d8c95d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349994} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8ca5a1-1a42-5caa-9222-f008dad6ec19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349995} {:answer/id #uuid "ef182f3a-9301-529c-a79c-bb644879b044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349996} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa7c714-4915-5a03-9d14-2998b5a58a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca74564e-978c-5d2a-9612-2fc106534d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349993} :answer-field/id #uuid "691de6ce-d5d3-5a13-9c50-06484df7668f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2188e54-85fe-53d7-875d-fe88e450703e" :question/math-academy-id "q-68043" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6eb0d88-2fd2-52de-91bc-9af61b9158cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326265} {:answer/id #uuid "924594b4-d799-5e7c-a60f-20c7a2a4171f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326266} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4794d1-6bd6-5787-9028-04a689758f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326267} {:answer/id #uuid "fde2a93d-c6f0-5365-b631-52802265652a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326268} {:answer/id #uuid "fc37606b-7354-5384-9aca-4d36dd8a52f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924594b4-d799-5e7c-a60f-20c7a2a4171f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326266} :answer-field/id #uuid "fadd03e5-8730-58cb-8eaa-be2b9f7fc337" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ec9156e-6a13-5769-a47f-7384d9da375e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75662" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- $AB=BA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8d65038-7933-5b48-accc-652c62fc2c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350000} {:answer/id #uuid "3e83efc3-a4a2-5c02-8a23-b7a1e0d380ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001} {:answer/id #uuid "c37427f1-19d6-5db5-b6d1-e700452a786c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350002} {:answer/id #uuid "74fb9603-13fb-5c0b-be0d-368e1781813f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350003} {:answer/id #uuid "7dae45a0-a246-5b2d-887e-38876039e3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e83efc3-a4a2-5c02-8a23-b7a1e0d380ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350001} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e608aba-7cc3-5f14-9c32-5d30b1a554af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af54b629-4ba1-522d-8445-a7561dd015c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-87685" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $1-4\\sqrt{3x-2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4\\sqrt{3x-2} & \\le 9 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{3x-2} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x-2} & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-2),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n3x-2\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84b3697-9ac6-510f-84f5-e6d0f172fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y<6 \\\\ y>2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350007} {:answer/id #uuid "116db77a-0c72-5c7e-828f-bdddfced3d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\le 6 \\\\ y\\le 2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350008} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e0db47-0c4d-559a-bb85-82354afa7bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\le 6 \\\\ y\\ge 2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350009} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ffc9fd-e1dd-5559-bfe8-c77d3a0de026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\ge 6 \\\\ y\\ge 2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350010} {:answer/id #uuid "4058b86a-60d4-5fb5-a126-6e52a54f592b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\ge 6 \\\\ y\\le 2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "116db77a-0c72-5c7e-828f-bdddfced3d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\le 6 \\\\ y\\le 2-x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350008} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54292a5-bfce-5666-9990-c08fe65c74db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3c44cb-d266-5c4d-b164-e3eeaaa99800" :question/math-academy-id "q-90056" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064989/assets/2b2f637d9468aa104dff160b1ebf1f764868e14704adc02e870ea19fa12e4b17.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $x+3y=6.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies  *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" or equal to inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{3}x+2,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+3y\\le 6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2-x.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le 2-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\le 6 \\\\ y\\le 2-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
